{"type":"standard","title":"Naukan Yupik language","displaytitle":"<span lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><span class=\"mw-page-title-main\">Naukan Yupik language</span></span>","namespace":{"id":0,"text":""},"wikibase_item":"Q27963","titles":{"canonical":"Naukan_Yupik_language","normalized":"Naukan Yupik language","display":"<span lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><span class=\"mw-page-title-main\">Naukan Yupik language</span></span>"},"pageid":3349051,"thumbnail":{"source":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c8/Asian_Siberian_Yupik_Eskimo_map.svg/330px-Asian_Siberian_Yupik_Eskimo_map.svg.png","width":330,"height":330},"originalimage":{"source":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c8/Asian_Siberian_Yupik_Eskimo_map.svg/960px-Asian_Siberian_Yupik_Eskimo_map.svg.png","width":900,"height":900},"lang":"en","dir":"ltr","revision":"1348784964","tid":"cbfbc68f-37d6-11f1-884a-d6eae6ec0413","timestamp":"2026-04-14T07:51:51Z","description":"Eskimo–Aleut language spoken in Russia","description_source":"local","content_urls":{"desktop":{"page":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Naukan_Yupik_language","revisions":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Naukan_Yupik_language?action=history","edit":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Naukan_Yupik_language?action=edit","talk":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Talk:Naukan_Yupik_language"},"mobile":{"page":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Naukan_Yupik_language","revisions":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Special:History/Naukan_Yupik_language","edit":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Naukan_Yupik_language?action=edit","talk":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Talk:Naukan_Yupik_language"}},"extract":"Naukan Yupik language or Naukan Siberian Yupik language is a critically endangered Eskaleut language spoken by c. 70 Naukan persons (нывуӄаӷмит) on the Chukotka peninsula. It is one of the four Yupik languages, along with Central Siberian Yupik, Central Alaskan Yup'ik and Pacific Gulf Yupik.","extract_html":"<p><b>Naukan Yupik language</b> or <b>Naukan Siberian Yupik language</b> is a critically endangered Eskaleut language spoken by c. 70 Naukan persons (<i>нывуӄаӷмит</i>) on the Chukotka peninsula. It is one of the four Yupik languages, along with Central Siberian Yupik, Central Alaskan Yup'ik and Pacific Gulf Yupik.</p>"}